sections.wikitext:
"Mrs. Robinson" was awarded two [[Grammy Awards]] at the [[11th Annual Grammy Awards]] in 1969. It became the first [[rock music|rock]] song to win [[Grammy Award for Record of the Year|Record of the Year]] (although the previous year's "[[Up, Up and Away (song)|Up Up and Away]]" by [[the 5th Dimension]] could also be considered a contender) and it also was awarded the Grammy for [[Grammy Award for Best Pop Performance by a Duo or Group with Vocals|Best Contemporary-Pop Performance – Vocal Duo or Group]].{{sfn|Eliot|2010|p=96}} Simon & Garfunkel were asked to perform the song live at the ceremony, but they declined. Instead, they shot a video for the show set to the music that consisted of them "romping around [[Yankee Stadium (1923)|Yankee Stadium]]", a reference to the song's lyrics concerning DiMaggio.{{sfn|Eliot|2010|p= 96}}

Since "Mrs. Robinson" was not written specifically for ''The Graduate'', it was deemed ineligible for the [[Academy Award for Best Original Song]].{{sfn|Eliot|2010|p=96}}

In 1999 the 1968 recording of the song on [[Columbia Records]] was inducted into the [[Grammy Hall of Fame]].<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.grammy.com/awards/hall-of-fame-award#m | title=Grammy Hall Of Fame - Hall of Fame Artists | website=Grammy Awards | access-date=December 29, 2023}}</ref>

